  "why": [
   "Kick and bass take turns. The median reference plays 69% of its bass notes off the beat and only 31% together with the kick, so each owns its moment in the low end: the sub stays clean and the groove bounces between them.",
   "Basslines lean on the root: degrees 1 30%, 4 22%, 3 8%, b3 7%. Staying on 1 and 5 keeps the low end stable under harmony that can drift above it.",
   "The kick is four on the floor in 100% of the references (typical x...x...x...x...): the constant pulse that lets everything else be syncopated.",
   "Clap/snare on 2 and 4 in 100% of them (typical mid-percussion xgxgxgxxxgxgxgxx): the backbeat marks the bar for dancers.",
   "Hats swing: median 57% (middle half 52-63%), timing spread 2 ms. Late off-beat 16ths are what make a straight 4/4 feel played rather than programmed.",
   "Harmony is patient: 0.4 chord changes per 8 bars (median), mostly m9, maj9, m11, 7sus4. Rich but slow chords colour the groove without pulling focus from it.",
   "Breaks work by subtraction: the kick stays in 0% of breaks, the bass in 52%, chords/pads in 90%, vocals in 57%. Taking the pulse away builds the tension the drop releases.",
   "The track unfolds slowly: bass enters at bar 17, chords/stabs at bar 9 (medians). DJs need those drum bars to mix in, and each entry is an event.",
   "Balance: bass -6 dB, chords/stabs -11 dB and vocals -20 dB against the drums (medians). The drums lead; harmony colours from behind."
  ]
 }
